        "text": "Subsecciones de: Chapter 11: Working with Virtual Machines\nP\u00e1ginas: 438-465\n\u2022 Technical requirements  (438)\n\u2022 Introduction to virtualization on Linux  (438)\n  \u2022 Efficiency in resource usage  (439)\n  \u2022 Introduction to hypervisors  (440)\n\u2022 Understanding Linux KVMs  (441)\n  \u2022 Choosing the hypervisor  (441)\n  \u2022 Using the KVM hypervisor  (442)\n\u2022 Working with basic KVM commands  (445)\n  \u2022 Creating a VM using the command line  (445)\n  \u2022 Basic VM management  (447)\n\u2022 Advanced KVM management  (448)\n  \u2022 Connecting to a VM  (449)\n  \u2022 Cloning VMs  (452)\n  \u2022 Creating VM templates  (453)\n  \u2022 Obtaining VM and host resource information  (454)\n  \u2022 Managing VM resource usage  (456)\n\u2022 Provisioning VMs using cloud-init  (457)\n  \u2022 Understanding how cloud-init works  (457)\n  \u2022 Installing and configuring cloud-init  (458)\n\u2022 Public key authentication with SSH  (461)\n\u2022 Summary  (463)\n\u2022 Exercises  (463)\n\u2022 Further reading  (464)\n11\nWorking with Virtual Machines\nIn this chapter, you will learn about virtual machines ( VMs ) on Linux. For starters, you will learn\nhow virtualization works and how to create and use VMs. You will learn about one of the most widely\nused virtualization and hypervisor technologies on Linux, called Kernel-based Virtual Machine\n( KVM ). The topics in this chapter will prepare you for the future of Linux, as it is the foundation of\nevery modern cloud technology. If you wish to remain up to date in a constantly changing landscape,\nthis chapter will be an essential starting point for your journey.\nIn this chapter, we\u2019re going to cover the following main topics:\n\u2022I Introduction to virtualization on Linux\n\u2022I Understanding Linux KVM\n\u2022I Working with basic KVM commands\n\u2022I Advanced KVM management\n\u2022I Provisioning VMs using cloud-init\n\u2022I Public key authentication with SSH",
